{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5","id":"msg_011CdsYVKbCqGBiPKHhuaogw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01K1K23nomL2Ln4PqDXYn1Bh","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-creator-0/113f7cc5-237e-5d6a-8867-a97fd89c58dd/scratchpad/e6/e6-t11/primary/exec-ws/input-class-analysis.md","content":"# Input-class analysis for e6-t11 (evidence requirement E-A, with provisional verdict E-C)\n\nAuthored at harness node `propose-child` by worker X-E6, from the sole package\nartifact `package/artifacts/dateadd.py`. Domain under audit: ISO dates `d` in\nyears 2023–2025, integer `n` with `0 <= n <= 60`. Claim: `add_days(d, n)`\nequals the true Gregorian date `n` days after `d`, for every such `(d, n)`.\n\n## 1. Source inspection\n\n`add_days` parses the ISO string, then increments the day `n` times; on each\nincrement it compares the day against `_dim(y, m)`, rolling the month (and the\nyear at month 12) when the day exceeds the month length. Month lengths come\nfrom:\n\n```python\nDAYS = [31, 28, 31, 30, 31, 30, 31, 30, 30, 31, 30, 31]   # as shipped\n#        Jan Feb Mar Apr May Jun Jul Aug Sep Oct Nov Dec\n```\n\nThe true Gregorian month-length table is:\n\n```text\n        Jan Feb Mar Apr May Jun Jul Aug Sep Oct Nov Dec\ntrue    31  28  31  30  31  30  31  31  30  31  30  31\nshipped 31  28  31  30  31  30  31  30  30  31  30  31\n                                     ^^\n```\n\nEvery entry matches the true table **except index 7 (August), which is 30\ninstead of 31**. `_leap` implements the full Gregorian rule (div-4 except\ncenturies unless div-400) and is correct; within 2023–2025 only `2024 % 4 == 0`\nmatters and evaluates correctly to leap.\n\n## 2. Partition of the domain into truth-relevant input classes\n\nThe claim's truth can turn only on: which month-length constant governs each\nrollover, whether the leap branch fires for February, whether month/year\nrollover arithmetic is right, and the parse/format round-trip. That yields the\nfollowing partition; together the classes exercise every structural case the\nclaim quantifies over.\n\n- **C0 — identity (n = 0).** The loop body never runs; output must equal the\n  (re-formatted) input. Exercises only parse/format. Representative: any `d`.\n- **C1 — intra-month advance, no boundary crossed.** `d + n` stays inside the\n  starting month; exercises the `d > _dim(y, m)` comparison on the false\n  branch only. Representative: `(\"2023-03-10\", 5)`.\n- **C2 — single month-boundary rollover, one subclass per month m = 1..12.**\n  Start at the true last day of month `m`, `n = 1`. Each subclass exercises\n  exactly the month-length constant `DAYS[m-1]` (or the leap branch for\n  February): the output is wrong iff that constant is wrong. All twelve\n  subclasses are reachable with `n <= 60` from dates inside 2023–2025; the\n  December subclass coincides with C4. Representatives:\n  `(\"2023-01-31\", 1)`, `(\"2023-02-28\", 1)`, `(\"2023-03-31\", 1)`,\n  `(\"2023-04-30\", 1)`, `(\"2023-05-31\", 1)`, `(\"2023-06-30\", 1)`,\n  `(\"2023-07-31\", 1)`, `(\"2023-08-31\", 1)`, `(\"2023-09-30\", 1)`,\n  `(\"2023-10-31\", 1)`, `(\"2023-11-30\", 1)`, `(\"2023-12-31\", 1)`.\n  **Boundary-approach refinement:** for each month also the class of starts on\n  the true penultimate day (`n = 1` must NOT roll over), which detects a\n  too-short constant that the last-day subclass alone can miss — e.g. Aug:\n  `(\"2023-08-30\", 1)` must yield `2023-08-31`, not roll into September.\n- **C3 — February leap distinction.** Leap year 2024:\n  `(\"2024-02-28\", 1)` → `2024-02-29` and `(\"2024-02-29\", 1)` → `2024-03-01`;\n  non-leap 2023/2025: `(\"2023-02-28\", 1)` and `(\"2025-02-28\", 1)` → `03-01`.\n  Exercises the `_leap` branch both ways within the domain.\n- **C4 — year rollover.** `(\"2023-12-31\", 1)`, `(\"2024-12-31\", 1)`,\n  `(\"2025-12-31\", 1)` → January 1 of the next year (the 2025 case exits the\n  domain years in its *output*, which the claim still covers since `d` is in\n  domain). Exercises `m > 12` reset and `y += 1`.\n- **C5 — multi-month spans, n up to 60.** Spans crossing two consecutive\n  boundaries; the output is wrong iff the sum of the two governing constants\n  is wrong. E.g. `(\"2024-01-31\", 60)` (Jan→Feb-leap→Mar),\n  `(\"2023-07-15\", 60)` (Jul→Aug→Sep), `(\"2025-11-30\", 32)` (Nov→Dec→Jan,\n  spanning the year boundary with n > 1). With `n <= 60` at most two\n  boundaries can be crossed, so C2+C5 cover all reachable boundary counts.\n- **C6 — century leap logic (vacuous in domain).** The `y % 100` / `y % 400`\n  sub-conditions of `_leap` are unreachable for any year touched from\n  2023–2025 with `n <= 60` (years touched: 2023–2026, none a century year).\n  The claim's truth cannot turn on them; recorded for completeness.\n- **C7 — parse/format round-trip.** Zero-padded `%04d-%02d-%02d` formatting,\n  exercised by every input; correct by inspection for all in-domain values.\n\nCoverage argument: every month-length constant is exercised by its C2\nsubclass (February via C3, in both leap and non-leap years), every month\nboundary reachable with `n <= 60` is crossed by C2/C5, the year boundary is\ncrossed by C4 for each domain year, `n = 0` is C0, and multi-month behavior up\nto the maximum reachable two boundaries is C5. Hence the partition covers\nevery case on which the claim's truth could turn.\n\n## 3. Provisional verdict (E-C): VIOLATED\n\nWitness: `[\"2023-08-30\", 1]` — a member of the C2 August boundary-approach\nsubclass, inside the domain (2023, n = 1).\n\nMechanical trace of `add_days(\"2023-08-30\", 1)`:\n\n1. Parse: `y = 2023, m = 8, d = 30`.\n2. Single loop iteration: `d = 31`; `_dim(2023, 8)` → `m != 2` → `DAYS[7]` =\n   **30**; `31 > 30` is true → `d = 1, m = 9`; `9 > 12` false.\n3. Format: `\"2023-09-01\"`.\n\nTrue Gregorian: August has 31 days, so one day after 2023-08-30 is\n**2023-08-31**. `add_days` returns `2023-09-01` ≠ `2023-08-31` → the claim is\nviolated at `[\"2023-08-30\", 1]`.\n\nCorroborating members of the same defect class (all in domain):\n`(\"2024-08-30\", 1)` → returns `2024-09-01`, true `2024-08-31`;\n`(\"2023-08-01\", 30)` → returns `2023-09-01`, true `2023-08-31`;\nany span from a start day `<= Aug 30` that passes the skipped `08-31` lands\none day later than the true date thereafter. Note the adjacent input\n`(\"2023-08-31\", 1)` happens to return the *correct* `2023-09-01`, because the\nearly rollover only skips day 31 when approached from below — which is why the\nboundary-approach refinement in C2 is part of the truth-relevant partition.\n\nAll other classes check out against the true calendar: every non-August C2\nsubclass rolls at the true month length, C3 leap behavior is correct for\n2023–2025, C4 year rollover is correct, and C5 spans not passing through an\nAugust 31st are consistent.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":898,"cache_read_input_tokens":44451,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":898},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cf679082-cce6-49d5-938c-d64ff2bb6aee","uuid":"a8b2ca22-9d6d-4221-82d4-27c4f96627a0","timestamp":"2026-08-09T17:11:34.313Z","request_id":"req_011CdsYVHj67K5ZEFMg53XuN"}
